\date{2026-01-21}
\title{What is \em{Discrete Mathematics}?}
\tag{draft}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Throughout your elementary, middle school, and high school years, XXX (1) math is linear (2) focused on reaching Calculus.  All about the \em{continuous}, \ie things that change smoothly. Both are false!  Math is not a linear chain of ideas, but rather an interconnected web.  And there are whole areas of math that fall, not under continuous, but \em{discrete} or algebraic.  Things that don't vary smoothly: jump from one to the next; collections of distinct things.  Broadly speaking, this is the kind of mathematics that is needed for computing: computers do not deal with continuous values but with discrete ones and zeros.}
